+# Product Requirements Document (PRD)
+
+**Project**: Pulseboard  
+**Version**: 1.0.0  
+**Date**: 2026-08-11  
+**Status**: Planning  
+**Scale**: Level 2
+
+---
+
+## Information Sources
+
+This plan is derived solely from the supplied project foundation:
+
+- `project.md` — pilot intent, success targets, and non-goals.
+- `product-contract.md` — PRM-001 through PRM-004.
+- `evidence-contract.md` — required end-to-end, isolation, and facilitator-LARP proof.
+- `context.md` — four-week, two-engineer Python API and browser UI constraints.
+
+No architecture, UX strategy, research report, or provider-selection decision was supplied. This PRD therefore makes no market-size or competitive claims, treats reminder-provider selection as a bounded implementation decision, and leaves detailed architecture to later epic planning.
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+Pulseboard helps a meeting facilitator turn an uploaded transcript into action items that are reviewed before they become commitments. The pilot product proposes actions, requires the facilitator to explicitly confirm the action text, owner, and due date, then sends one reminder using a configurable provider and shows whether delivery succeeded. Workspace isolation is a core product requirement: one workspace must not be able to read another workspace's transcripts or actions.
+
+The four-week pilot is successful when five teams use it, at least 80% of proposed actions are reviewed, and those teams have 30% fewer unowned overdue actions.
+
+## Product Vision & Strategy
+
+### Vision Statement
+
+Make meeting follow-through dependable without removing human judgment. Pulseboard converts the raw material of a transcript into reviewed, owned, dated actions; it does not autonomously send commitments or replace the calendar system teams already use.
+
+### Strategic Objectives
+
+1. **Turn transcript content into reviewable proposals**: facilitators can upload a transcript and receive proposed action items.
+2. **Preserve facilitator control before any commitment**: an item cannot be scheduled until its text, owner, and due date have each been explicitly confirmed.
+3. **Close the loop once**: a confirmed item receives one reminder through a configurable provider, with delivery status visible to the facilitator.
+4. **Make the pilot safe for multiple teams**: workspace-scoped requests prevent cross-workspace reads.
+
+### Success Definition
+
+- **Pilot adoption**: five teams use Pulseboard during the four-week pilot.
+- **Review behavior**: at least 80% of proposed action items are reviewed by a facilitator.
+- **Operational outcome**: participating teams have 30% fewer unowned overdue actions over four weeks.
+- **Trust boundary**: real-request-role negative testing demonstrates cross-workspace transcript and action access is denied.
+
+## Users and Core Journey
+
+### Primary Persona: Meeting Facilitator
+
+- **Goal**: leave a meeting with clear, owned, dated follow-through.
+- **Pain point**: actions mentioned in transcripts are easy to overlook or remain unowned.
+- **Success looks like**: they can review suggestions quickly, correct them where needed, and know whether the one intended reminder was delivered.
+
+### Core Journey: Transcript to Confirmed Reminder
+
+1. The facilitator uploads a meeting transcript within their workspace.
+2. Pulseboard extracts proposed action items from that transcript.
+3. The facilitator reviews each proposal and explicitly confirms or corrects its text, owner, and due date.
+4. Only after all three fields are explicitly confirmed does the item become eligible for scheduling.
+5. Pulseboard sends one reminder through the workspace's configured provider and displays delivery status.
+
+## Functional Requirements
+
+### FR-1: Transcript-backed proposals (PRM-001)
+
+- A facilitator can upload a transcript to their workspace.
+- The system creates proposed action items from that transcript for facilitator review.
+- A proposal remains a proposal until it is explicitly confirmed; extraction alone creates no scheduled reminder.
+
+### FR-2: Explicit confirmation gate (PRM-002)
+
+- A facilitator must explicitly confirm the action text, owner, and due date before scheduling is permitted.
+- The interface makes incomplete confirmation visible and prevents the scheduling path from bypassing a missing confirmation.
+- Corrections made during review are the values used for the confirmed action.
+
+### FR-3: One reminder with visible outcome (PRM-003)
+
+- A workspace can configure the provider used for the pilot reminder path.
+- A confirmed action receives one reminder through that provider.
+- The facilitator can see the delivery status for that reminder.
+- Provider selection is not locked in this plan; implementation must preserve configurability.
+
+### FR-4: Workspace isolation (PRM-004)
+
+- Transcripts, proposals, confirmed actions, and reminder status are scoped to a workspace.
+- The real request role cannot read another workspace's transcripts or actions.
+
+### Explicit Non-goals
+
+- Recording meetings.
+- Replacing calendars.
+- Autonomous outbound messaging.
+- Multiple reminders, reminder campaigns, or escalation sequences.
+- Selecting or contracting a specific reminder provider during project planning.
+
+## Non-functional Requirements
+
+### Security and Data Boundaries
+
+- Enforce workspace isolation for transcripts and actions.
+- Prove isolation with a negative probe using the real request role, as required by the evidence contract.
+- The supplied context excludes regulated health and payment data; no compliance claim beyond the supplied scope is made here.
+
+### Usability and Quality
+
+- The browser UI must support the facilitator review journey without hiding whether confirmation is complete or whether delivery succeeded.
+- Validation must include a facilitator LARP, not only automated checks.
+
+### Delivery Constraints
+
+- Build a Python API and browser UI.
+- Plan for two engineers and a four-week pilot.
+- Keep provider integration configurable because the provider is not yet selected.
+
+## Release Strategy
+
+### Pilot MVP
+
+The MVP is the complete controlled path: upload a transcript, review extracted proposals, explicitly confirm text/owner/due date, send one provider-backed reminder, and inspect delivery status. Workspace isolation and proof of that boundary are included in MVP, not deferred hardening.
+
+### Delivery Phases
+
+1. **Foundation (E000)**: establish repeatable local and CI checks, environment configuration, and test support needed for a reliable pilot build.
+2. **Review path (E001)**: deliver workspace-scoped transcript upload, proposed actions, and the explicit confirmation gate.
+3. **Reminder closure (E002)**: add configurable provider delivery and visible status to confirmed actions; verify the end-to-end path.
+
+## Success Metrics and Measurement
+
+| Metric | Target | Measurement |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Teams using the pilot | 5 teams | Pilot workspace roster / usage record |
+| Proposal review rate | >=80% | Reviewed proposals divided by all proposals during the pilot |
+| Unowned overdue actions | 30% fewer | Compare the participating teams' four-week pilot period with the agreed baseline |
+| Reminder delivery visibility | Every attempted pilot reminder has a recorded status | Runtime evidence and delivery-status records |
+| Workspace isolation | No cross-workspace read in the real-role negative probe | Checked-in integration evidence |
+
+The baseline definition and analytics implementation are delivery details to be specified in the relevant epics; this plan does not invent an unavailable baseline or vendor.
+
+## Risks and Mitigations
+
+| Risk | Impact | Mitigation | Owner |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Extracted proposals are inaccurate | Facilitators lose trust | Keep proposals reviewable and require explicit confirmation before scheduling | E001 |
+| Provider behavior is uncertain | Reminder delivery cannot be completed | Isolate the provider behind a configurable integration and choose it during E002 planning | E002 |
+| Workspace data leaks | Core trust requirement fails | Scope data access by workspace and run the real-role negative probe | E001 |
+| Four-week schedule is tight | Pilot scope expands beyond delivery capacity | Limit MVP to one reminder and retain stated non-goals | Project planning |
+
+## Dependencies
+
+### External Dependencies
+
+- A reminder provider, selected during E002 planning, for the one-reminder delivery path.
+
+### Internal Dependencies
+
+- E000 enables reliable checks and environment handling for feature work.
+- E002 depends on E001's confirmed, workspace-scoped actions.
+
+## Glossary
+
+| Term | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| Transcript | The uploaded meeting text from which Pulseboard proposes actions. |
+| Proposal | An extracted action item that has not yet been explicitly confirmed. |
+| Confirmed action | An action whose text, owner, and due date have all been explicitly confirmed by a facilitator. |
+| Workspace | The isolation boundary for a team's transcripts and actions. |
+| Delivery status | The recorded outcome of the single reminder attempt. |
+
+## Traceability
+
+| Promise | Planned epic | Requirement coverage |
+|---|---|---|
+| PRM-001 | E001 | FR-1 |
+| PRM-002 | E001 | FR-2 |
+| PRM-003 | E002 | FR-3 |
+| PRM-004 | E001 | FR-4 |
+
